      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251656",
      ":question/problem": "| $x_{i}$ | $y_{i}$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $127$ | $130$ |\n| $120$ | $122$ |\n| $115$ | $118$ |\n| $132$ | $131$ |\n| $122$ | $122$ |\n\nA clinical trial measured the systolic blood pressure of a sample of five participants before and after they received a new treatment for chronic pain. The results of the observations (in $mmHg$) are represented in the table above. The differences between the two observations are normally distributed with unknown means and variance $\u03bc$ and $\u03c3^{2}$, respectively. The head of the trial wishes to carry out a hypothesis test at the $10%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the participants' systolic blood pressure has changed after the treatment.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n| $k\u2216p$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $4$ | $1.533$ | $2.132$ |\n| $5$ | $1.476$ | $2.015$ |\n| $6$ | $1.440$ | $1.943$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $10%$ **two-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $-2.015$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}\n\nAt the $10%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-4}}\nevidence that the participants' systolic blood pressure has changed after the treatment.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
    },
