      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-251725",
      ":question/problem": "A researcher tested the effect of a sleep app on the sleep quality score of $38$ individuals. The sleep quality score (out of $10$) was recorded before and after participants had used the app for $3$ months.\nThe mean and variance of the differences in the observations are $d^(\u2015) =-0.8$ and $s^{2} = 2.56$, respectively.\nThe researcher wishes to carry out a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to determine whether there is sufficient evidence that the sleep app improved sleep quality.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k, p}$ such that $P(T > t_{k, p}) = p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n| $k\u2216p$ | $0.010$ | $0.025$ | $0.050$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $37$ | $2.431$ | $2.026$ | $1.687$ |\n| $38$ | $2.429$ | $2.024$ | $1.686$ |\n| $39$ | $2.426$ | $2.023$ | $1.685$ |\n\nUsing this information, select the options that make the following statements true.\n\nThe test statistic for the chosen test is $t \\approx$\n{{field-1}}.\n\nThe $1%$ **left-tailed** critical region for the test statistic is approximately $T \\le$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nAt the $1%$ level of significance, there is\n{{field-3}}\nevidence that the sleep app improved sleep quality.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."
